[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergences found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Symbol parity and the happy path (`good`, `driver(1)`, `printIntPtrLine` with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m valid pointer) were already identical before this verification pass. All three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m real divergences were on the `bad()` path, and none of them would have been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m visible to per-function happy-path tests.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `driver(0)` — optimized tail call instead of a real frame
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` was translated as idiomatic Rust and LLVM compiled it to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `test %edi,%edi; je ...; jmp bad`. A tail jump means `bad` executes with **no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` frame beneath it**, so its uninitialized read of `-0x8(%rbp)` lands 32
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes higher than in the C, which builds a `-O0` frame and issues a real
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `call bad`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed, same harness, same input, `driver(0)`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:    prints stale stack data, exit 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fixed by emitting all four functions as naked functions reproducing GCC's `-O0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instruction sequence, so the frame sizes, spill offsets and call/tail-call
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m structure match exactly. Caught by 10 tests; `cfg_20_bad_direct_vs_via_driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m localises it precisely (direct call reads poison slot *n*, via-`driver` reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *n+4*; the tail-jumping version read *n* in both cases).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Eager vs lazy PLT binding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C `.so` is linked with the system compiler's default — partial RELRO with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **lazy** PLT binding. rustc defaults to full RELRO / `-z now`. Normally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m invisible; not here, because the first `call bad@plt` under lazy binding detours
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through `_dl_runtime_resolve`, which consumes a large amount of stack below the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caller's frame — precisely the bytes `bad()` then reads.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed, `driver(0)` three times in one process:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:    1137197056   608471368   608471368
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:  608471368   608471368   608471368
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The two agreed from the second call onward and disagreed only on the first — the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one that ran the resolver in the lazily-bound C library. Fixed in `build.rs` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-Wl,-z,lazy`. Guarded by `link_mode_matches_c` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `lazy_binding_first_call_is_not_reproducible_even_c_vs_c`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. `std` linkage changing the loader's stack footprint
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With both libraries lazily bound, a further systematic difference appeared. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `.so` records one `NEEDED` entry and a 43-entry `.dynsym`; the std-linked Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cdylib` recorded three (`libgcc_s.so.1`, `libc.so.6`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`) and 1003. The resolver's stack consumption scales with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the symbol-lookup work, so the residue it left differed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 40 lazily-bound runs of driver(0):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   C:    0/40 faults
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Rust: 40/40 faults (SIGSEGV)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fixed by making the crate `#![no_std]` (nothing here needs an allocator or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unwinding — the four functions are naked assembly plus one `printf` import) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m recording the same `libc.so.6` dependency the C library does. The Rust `.so` went
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from 402 696 bytes / 1003 dynsym / 3 `NEEDED` to 6 416 / 44 / 1, and the fault
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rates now match at 0/40. Guarded by `elf_02_dependency_parity`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `elf_03_dynsym_size_comparable` and `lazy_binding_fault_behaviour_matches`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that whether a *particular* std-linked build faults is sensitive to the whole
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m link configuration, not to `std` alone; this is why two of the three guards check
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the ELF-level cause directly rather than relying only on the observable symptom.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### What remains genuinely uncomparable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Under lazy binding, the value the **first** `driver(0)` prints is derived from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ASLR-randomised addresses the resolver leaves on the stack. It is not reproducible
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m even when the C library is compared against *itself* — 40 runs produced 40
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinct values. No translation can be required to match it, so the differential
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparisons run with `LD_BIND_NOW=1`, where the value is fully deterministic and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m does match. `lazy_binding_first_call_is_not_reproducible_even_c_vs_c` asserts the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-reproducibility rather than assuming it, so that if the loader ever becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deterministic here the test fails and tells us to compare the case directly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproducing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && ./verify.sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `verify.sh` builds the C library, enumerates `[features]` from `Cargo.toml`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs the suite for every combination under both profiles, then checks symbol
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m parity, unresolved symbols, per-function instruction streams and the ELF profile.
