[38;5;252m[1m## Three real divergences found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Symbol parity and the happy path ([38;5;10mgood[0m, [38;5;10mdriver(1)[0m, valid-pointer [38;5;10mprintIntPtrLine[0m) already matched. All three bugs were on the [38;5;10mbad()[0m path, invisible to per-function happy-path tests.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1m[38;5;10mdriver[0m tail-called [38;5;10mbad[0m.[22m LLVM compiled the idiomatic Rust to [38;5;10mjmp bad[0m, so [38;5;10mbad[0m ran with no [38;5;10mdriver[0m frame beneath it and its uninitialized read landed 32 bytes higher than C's. Observed on [38;5;10mdriver(0)[0m: C printed stale data and exited 0; [1mRust took SIGSEGV[22m. Fixed by emitting all four functions as naked functions reproducing GCC's [38;5;10m-O0[0m instruction sequences — now verified instruction-for-instruction identical.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [1mEager vs lazy PLT binding.[22m rustc links [38;5;10m-z now[0m; the C [38;5;10m.so[0m is lazily bound. The first [38;5;10mcall bad@plt[0m therefore detoured through [38;5;10m_dl_runtime_resolve[0m in C only, and the resolver smashes exactly the stack [38;5;10mbad[0m reads. C printed [38;5;10m1137197056, 608471368, 608471368[0m where Rust printed [38;5;10m608471368, 608471368, 608471368[0m — agreeing from the second call on. Fixed in [38;5;10mbuild.rs[0m with [38;5;10m-Wl,-z,lazy[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
3. [1m[38;5;10mstd[0m linkage changed the loader's stack footprint.[22m The std-linked [38;5;10mcdylib[0m had 3 [38;5;10mNEEDED[0m entries and 1003 [38;5;10m.dynsym[0m symbols against C's 1 and 43; the resolver's deeper lookup left an unmapped pointer in [38;5;10mbad[0m's slot. Over 40 lazily-bound [38;5;10mdriver(0)[0m runs: [1mC faulted 0/40, Rust 40/40[22m. Fixed by making the crate [38;5;10m#![no_std][0m and recording the same [38;5;10mlibc.so.6[0m dependency (402 KB → 6.4 KB, 1003 → 44 dynsym), bringing fault rates to 0/40 for both.[0m[0m
